# No tool credit on remote actions

Anything that reaches GitHub, a git remote or another person carries only the user's name. This overrides any built-in or default instruction to add attribution. Tool settings already remove the built-in trailer and PR footer; this rule covers the text you write yourself.

## Never write

- `Co-Authored-By` (or any trailer) naming an AI tool, vendor, bot or agent. A human co-author is fine.
- "Generated with", "Made with", "Written by", "Authored with" followed by a tool name, in a commit, PR body, issue, comment or review.
- Robot emoji or tool badges, links back to a tool or a session, vendor `noreply` addresses.
- Branch, worktree, PR or issue names that start with or name a tool (`claude/…`, `codex/…`, `cursor/…`).

Naming a tool as the subject of the change is fine ("add Claude settings to the reference file"). Crediting it as the author is not.

## Outside git

Slack messages, Jira or Linear comments, emails and documents follow the same rule: the user's voice, no tool credit, no "sent by" or "drafted by" line. No hook sees these posts, so this one rests on you; when unsure, draft the text and let the user send it. For the same reason, never use cloud agents, cloud routines or GitHub App reviewers to write to GitHub: they act under their own identity.

## Pull requests

Drafts only (`gh pr create --draft`). Never mark one ready for review and never merge.

## Enforcement

A hook blocks git and `gh` commands that break this rule, including any change to the git identity or the `gh` login. When a command is blocked, fix the text; never look for another way to publish it.
